            <span class="faq-plus text-slate-400 text-2xl font-light leading-none shrink-0 mt-0.5">+</span>
          </summary>
          <p class="text-slate-500 text-sm leading-relaxed mt-3 pl-10">The scan is automatic. SiteGov probes the domain and populates registrar, DNS provider, nameservers, hosting, SSL, and email infrastructure without manual entry. What you add is who controls each one: who has the login, MFA status, credential location. That part takes minutes per domain, once, and stays current.</p>
        </details>

        <details class="faq py-6">
          <summary class="flex items-start gap-5">
            <span class="text-slate-400 text-[10px] font-mono tracking-widest shrink-0 mt-1">Q&middot;04</span>
            <h3 class="flex-1 text-slate-900 font-semibold pr-4">What happens to our client&rsquo;s data if the relationship ends?</h3>
            <span class="faq-plus text-slate-400 text-2xl font-light leading-none shrink-0 mt-0.5">+</span>
          </summary>
          <p class="text-slate-500 text-sm leading-relaxed mt-3 pl-10">The governance record belongs to the domain owner, not the agency that built it. If the relationship ends, the record transfers to the client. It&rsquo;s not lost, and it&rsquo;s not locked in. Think of it like medical records: the provider maintains them, but they belong to the patient. Your client&rsquo;s infrastructure history is theirs to keep.</p>
